# ==============================================================================
# The AquariusOS boot screen — what it actually does
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# This is a little program. Plymouth — the program that draws the screen you see
# while the computer is starting up or shutting down — runs it, and everything on
# that screen is drawn by the steps below.
#
# It is written in Plymouth's own small scripting language. You have not seen it
# before and you do not need to know it: it looks like most other languages,
# `#` starts a comment, and every piece of it that is not obvious is explained
# where it happens.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR THINGS THIS SCREEN EVER SHOWS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. STARTING UP — "the pour".  AquariusOS is named after Aquarius, the
#      water-bearer, so the "A" is poured. A stream of water falls from above the
#      screen into the point at the top of the A; both legs run downward out of
#      it; the wave spills across from left to right; and the word "AquariusOS"
#      fades in underneath. 2.2 seconds. Then it HOLDS — stands still — until the
#      login screen takes over.
#
#   2. SHUTTING DOWN or RESTARTING — "the wind".  The opposite. The word fades,
#      then the wind takes the mark apart and blows it away to the right in
#      drops. 1.9 seconds, then the screen is dark.
#
#   3. INSTALLING UPDATES.  The pour, then the hold, and underneath it: what is
#      being installed, "Please do not turn the computer off.", a thin progress
#      bar, and a percentage. When it reaches the end the line changes to
#      "Turning off." and the wind plays.
#
#   4. UNLOCKING AN ENCRYPTED DISK.  The pour, then a box to type the password
#      into, one dot per character, and a line of plain words underneath. A
#      wrong password shakes the box once and says so.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW A "FRAME" WORKS, IF THAT IS NEW
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A moving picture is a stack of still pictures shown quickly one after another.
# Each still is a FRAME. The pour is 66 of them and the wind is 57, both played
# at 30 a second. They are ordinary picture files sitting in this same folder —
# boot-0001.png … boot-0066.png and shutdown-0001.png … shutdown-0057.png — and
# all this program does with them is show the right one at the right moment.
#
# The pictures are DRAWN ON THE MAC and committed to the repository, by
# branding/render-plymouth-assets.sh. Nothing here draws a shape; there is no
# way to draw a shape at boot time. If you want to change what the animation
# LOOKS like, that is branding/pour.mjs and that script — not this file. If you
# want to change what the boot screen DOES, you are in the right file.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ AFTER ANY CHANGE HERE, THE BOOT RAMDISK HAS TO BE REBUILT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A copy of this whole folder is baked into a file called the initramfs, and it
# is THAT copy the machine actually uses. The build rebuilds it for you in
# build_files/80-boot-branding.sh. If an edit ever appears to do nothing at all,
# that is the mechanism, and docs/restart/boot-branding.md explains it properly.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THE NAMES IN THIS FILE COME FROM
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything spelled `Plymouth.Something`, `Window.Something`, `Image` or
# `Sprite` is Plymouth's, not ours. They were read out of the source code of the
# exact Plymouth this image ships — version 24.004.60, the one in Fedora 44 —
# rather than copied from a tutorial, because these names have changed between
# releases and a misspelled one fails silently: the screen simply never does that
# thing. The files they were read from are
# src/plugins/splash/script/script-lib-{plymouth,image,sprite}.c in Plymouth's
# own source.
#
# A "sprite" is one picture placed at one spot on the screen. You make one, you
# give it a picture, you tell it where to sit and how see-through to be, and
# Plymouth draws it. Everything on this screen is a sprite.
# ==============================================================================


# ==============================================================================
# 1. THE NUMBERS, THE COLOURS AND THE WORDS
# ==============================================================================
# ⚠️ THE FRAME COUNTS AND THE FRAME SIZE MUST MATCH THREE OTHER FILES:
# branding/pour.mjs, branding/render-plymouth-assets.sh and the checks in
# build_files/80-boot-branding.sh. Change one, change all four. The build fails
# if they disagree, but it is quicker to get it right here.
BOOT_FRAMES  = 66;      # the pour: 2.2 seconds at 30 pictures a second
WIND_FRAMES  = 57;      # the wind: 1.9 seconds
# How long to wait, at boot only, before the pour begins: 45 pictures = 1.5 s.
# Found on the bench, 2026-09-07: the pour played to a television that was
# still showing the motherboard's logo. A big screen takes a second or two to
# switch over after the machine starts drawing, and by the time the picture
# arrived the pour was over and only the held mark was left. So at boot the
# screen stays plain navy for this long, then the stream falls. Shutdown is not
# delayed: the screen is already awake. (The login screen is also held back
# until the pour has finished — see aquarius-boot-hold.service.)
BOOT_DELAY   = 45;
FRAME_WIDTH  = 288;
FRAME_HEIGHT = 389;

BAR_WIDTH  = 320;       # the progress bar on the update screen
BAR_HEIGHT = 3;
BOX_WIDTH  = 420;       # the box you type a disk password into
BOX_HEIGHT = 48;

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Turning a colour into the numbers Plymouth wants
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Colours everywhere else in this project are written as six hex digits —
# #0B1220. Plymouth instead wants each of red, green and blue as a number
# between 0 and 1.
#
# So `channel` does the conversion: hand it the 0-to-255 value of one part of a
# colour and it hands back Plymouth's version of it.
#
# ⚠️ THE HALF IS NOT A ROUNDING ERROR, IT IS THE FIX FOR ONE. Plymouth multiplies
# the number back by 255 and throws the fraction away rather than rounding it. So
# 11/255 can come back as 10, and a colour that is one step out is a colour that
# is wrong. Adding half a step first means the number you write is the number
# that comes out.
fun channel (value_out_of_255)
  {
    return (value_out_of_255 + 0.5) / 255;
  }

# The Midnight palette, from branding/tokens.md. The boot screen is a dark
# screen, so it uses the same colours the desktop shows in dark mode — which is
# why the machine does not change colour between starting up and being started.
# Never pick one of these by eye.
#
#   bg        #0B1220 = 11, 18, 32     the ground, edge to edge
#   ink       #DCE9F4 = 220, 233, 244  headings
#   inkSoft   #93A7BC = 147, 167, 188  ordinary lines of explanation
#   inkMute   #5C6E82 = 92, 110, 130   the percentage, and anything quieter still
BG_RED    = channel(11);   BG_GREEN    = channel(18);   BG_BLUE    = channel(32);
INK_RED   = channel(220);  INK_GREEN   = channel(233);  INK_BLUE   = channel(244);
SOFT_RED  = channel(147);  SOFT_GREEN  = channel(167);  SOFT_BLUE  = channel(188);
MUTE_RED  = channel(92);   MUTE_GREEN  = channel(110);  MUTE_BLUE  = channel(130);

# The typefaces, exactly as the boot screen carried them before this rewrite.
# build_files/80-boot-branding.sh asks for these three font files to be put
# inside the boot ramdisk, and the build checks that they arrived — because if
# one is missing, Plymouth quietly falls back to whatever face it can find and
# nobody notices until they look at a photograph of the screen.
#
# The word "AquariusOS" under the mark is NOT set here. It is already part of
# the animation's pictures, drawn as a shape on the Mac, so it can never fall
# back to the wrong face.
TITLE_FONT   = "Sora Semi-Bold 30";
UNLOCK_FONT  = "Sora Semi-Bold 24";
BODY_FONT    = "Inter 16";
NUMBER_FONT  = "JetBrains Mono 11";

# The words on the update screens, one pair per kind of update. These are word
# for word what the old boot screen said — they were reviewed then and there is
# no reason to change them now.
DO_NOT_TURN_OFF = "Please do not turn the computer off.";
TURNING_OFF     = "Turning off.";

# The words on the disk-password screen.
UNLOCK_TITLE  = "Unlock the disk";
UNLOCK_HELP   = "Type the disk password to start.";
UNLOCK_WRONG  = "That password did not work. Try again.";
ANSWER_HELP   = "Type your answer, then press Enter.";

# How the box shakes when a password is wrong: one quick left-right-left, three
# nudges of six pixels and then two smaller ones to settle. Seven steps at 30 a
# second is a shake that lasts about a quarter of a second — long enough to
# notice, short enough not to be annoying.
SHAKE_STEPS = 7;
shake_offset[0] =  6;
shake_offset[1] = -6;
shake_offset[2] =  6;
shake_offset[3] = -6;
shake_offset[4] =  3;
shake_offset[5] = -3;
shake_offset[6] =  0;


# ==============================================================================
# 2. THE GROUND
# ==============================================================================
# A flat Midnight fill over the whole screen, painted before anything else and
# on every frame. Setting the top and the bottom to the same colour is how you
# ask for a flat fill rather than a gradient; the design's boot screen is flat.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS WHAT REMOVES THE COMPUTER-MAKER'S BADGE. A stock Fedora machine
# shows a Dell or ASUS logo while it starts, because Fedora's default boot theme
# exists to pick up the picture the computer's own firmware left in memory and
# use it as the background. Nothing in this file ever asks for that picture, and
# the plug-in running this script has no code to draw it, so it cannot appear.
Window.SetBackgroundTopColor(BG_RED, BG_GREEN, BG_BLUE);
Window.SetBackgroundBottomColor(BG_RED, BG_GREEN, BG_BLUE);

# How many times a second Plymouth should call us to draw the next frame. The
# animation was drawn at 30 a second, so asking for 30 means one call is exactly
# one frame and there is no arithmetic to get wrong. (Left alone Plymouth uses
# 50, which would play the animation too fast.)
Plymouth.SetRefreshRate(30);


# ==============================================================================
# 3. WORKING OUT WHERE THINGS GO
# ==============================================================================
# Positions here are worked out from the size of the screen rather than typed in
# as pixels, so that one file is right on a small laptop panel and on the 4K
# monitor on the bench.
#
# `Window.GetX()` and `Window.GetY()` are the top-left corner of the screen and
# `Window.GetWidth()` / `Window.GetHeight()` are its size. On a machine with two
# monitors these describe the whole arrangement, and Plymouth draws every sprite
# on every screen, so the mark appears centred on both.
#
# ⚠️ THESE ARE WORKED OUT ONCE, HERE, AND NOT AGAIN. Plymouth does offer to tell
# a theme when a monitor is plugged in or wakes up mid-boot
# (`Plymouth.SetDisplayHotplugFunction`) and this theme deliberately does not ask
# — the same choice Plymouth's own example theme makes. Handling it means
# re-placing every sprite on screen, which is a code path that only ever runs on
# a real machine at a moment nobody can arrange, and untested code that runs
# during boot is worse than a logo that is off-centre.
#
# So it is a BENCH QUESTION rather than a settled one: docs/restart/boot-branding.md
# asks for a look at the mark on the 4K monitor. If it is ever off-centre there,
# this is the reason and the fix is to add that callback.
fun screen_middle_x ()
  {
    return Window.GetX() + Window.GetWidth() / 2;
  }

# The mark sits with its middle 44% of the way down the screen — slightly above
# the true centre, because the space below it holds the update or password
# furniture and the two together read as balanced. It is the same 44% the old
# boot screen used, so the logo does not appear to jump between the two.
mark.x = screen_middle_x() - FRAME_WIDTH / 2;
mark.y = Window.GetY() + Window.GetHeight() * 0.44 - FRAME_HEIGHT / 2;

# Everything else hangs off the BOTTOM of the mark rather than off the screen, so
# nothing can ever land on top of the logo however tall or short the screen is.
below_mark = mark.y + FRAME_HEIGHT + 24;

# ⚠️ …unless the screen is genuinely tiny, in which case hanging things off the
# mark would push them off the bottom. The mark is 389 pixels tall and the
# furniture under it needs about 130 more, so on anything shorter than roughly
# 600 pixels the sum above runs past the edge — a fallback 640x480 mode being the
# real case. Rather than let the progress bar vanish, the whole group is pulled
# back up to sit against the bottom of the screen. It will overlap the mark on a
# screen that small, which is much better than being invisible.
FURNITURE_HEIGHT = 130;
screen_bottom = Window.GetY() + Window.GetHeight();
if (below_mark + FURNITURE_HEIGHT > screen_bottom)
  below_mark = screen_bottom - FURNITURE_HEIGHT;

# The update screen, top to bottom.
update.title_y   = below_mark;
update.line_y    = below_mark + 52;
update.bar_x     = screen_middle_x() - BAR_WIDTH / 2;
update.bar_y     = below_mark + 96;
update.percent_y = below_mark + 112;

# The disk-password screen, top to bottom.
dialog.title_y = below_mark;
dialog.box_x   = screen_middle_x() - BOX_WIDTH / 2;
dialog.box_y   = below_mark + 46;
dialog.help_y  = below_mark + 110;

# How far in front of each other things are drawn. Bigger is nearer the viewer.
# The mark is at the back, the update furniture in front of it, the password box
# in front of that, and a message from the system in front of everything.
Z_MARK    = 0;
Z_UPDATE  = 10;
Z_DIALOG  = 100;
Z_MESSAGE = 1000;


# ==============================================================================
# 4. LOADING THE PICTURES
# ==============================================================================
# Every picture the screen might show has to be loaded before it can be shown,
# and loading one takes a moment. So only the pictures the machine is actually
# going to need are loaded: a normal start-up loads the 66 frames of the pour and
# never touches the 57 of the wind.
#
# ⚠️ FOUR-DIGIT NUMBERS. The files are called boot-0001.png, not boot-1.png. This
# builds that name. Get it wrong and Plymouth looks for a file that is not there,
# finds nothing, and the frame is simply blank — no error, no warning.
fun four_digits (n)
  {
    if (n < 10)   return "000" + n;
    if (n < 100)  return "00" + n;
    if (n < 1000) return "0" + n;
    return "" + n;
  }

fun load_pour ()
  {
    if (animation.pour_loaded) return;
    for (i = 0; i < BOOT_FRAMES; i++)
      animation.pour[i] = Image("boot-" + four_digits(i + 1) + ".png");
    # What is left on screen once the pour has finished. It is the pour's last
    # frame, drawn from the same instant, and the render script proves the two
    # files are byte-for-byte identical — so there is no flicker at the moment
    # the animation stops.
    animation.hold = Image("hold.png");
    animation.pour_loaded = 1;
  }

fun load_wind ()
  {
    if (animation.wind_loaded) return;
    for (i = 0; i < WIND_FRAMES; i++)
      animation.wind[i] = Image("shutdown-" + four_digits(i + 1) + ".png");
    animation.wind_loaded = 1;
  }


# ==============================================================================
# 5. WHICH STORY TO PLAY
# ==============================================================================
# `Plymouth.GetMode()` says what the computer is doing. It is one of exactly
# these words, and they come from Plymouth's own source rather than from
# guesswork:
#
#   "boot"              starting up normally
#   "shutdown"          turning off
#   "reboot"            restarting
#   "updates"           installing updates before it turns off
#   "system-upgrade"    moving to a new version of the whole system
#   "firmware-upgrade"  updating the computer's own firmware
#   "system-reset"      resetting the machine
#   "unknown"           Plymouth could not tell (it does happen; treat it as a
#                       normal start-up rather than showing nothing at all)
animation.mode = Plymouth.GetMode();

# Going down: the wind. Everything else: the pour.
if (animation.mode == "shutdown" || animation.mode == "reboot")
  {
    animation.story = "wind";
    load_wind();
  }
else
  {
    animation.story = "pour";
    load_pour();
  }

animation.frame = 0;
animation.waited = 0;
animation.sprite = Sprite();
animation.sprite.SetPosition(mark.x, mark.y, Z_MARK);


# ==============================================================================
# 6. PLAYING IT
# ==============================================================================
# Plymouth calls `refresh_callback` 30 times a second, for as long as the boot
# screen is up. Each call moves everything on by exactly one frame. That is the
# whole animation: there is no timer and no clock, just "show the next one".
fun step_animation ()
  {
    if (animation.story == "wind")
      {
        if (animation.frame < WIND_FRAMES)
          {
            animation.sprite.SetImage(animation.wind[animation.frame]);
            animation.frame++;
          }
        else
          {
            # The wind has finished. Nothing is left on screen — which is the
            # point: the machine is going off.
            animation.sprite.SetOpacity(0);
          }
        return;
      }

    # The wait before the pour: nothing on screen but the navy, so the first
    # thing the person sees is the stream falling, not a frozen first frame.
    # BOOT_DELAY is explained beside BOOT_FRAMES at the top.
    if (animation.waited < BOOT_DELAY)
      {
        animation.waited++;
        return;
      }
    if (animation.frame < BOOT_FRAMES)
      {
        animation.sprite.SetImage(animation.pour[animation.frame]);
        animation.frame++;

        # The pour has just finished. Swap in the hold — the same picture as the
        # frame we have just shown, so nothing moves — and from now on this
        # function does nothing at all. The mark stands still until the login
        # screen takes over.
        if (animation.frame == BOOT_FRAMES)
          animation.sprite.SetImage(animation.hold);
      }
  }

fun refresh_callback ()
  {
    step_shake();       # the password box's one shake, if one is running
    step_animation();   # the pour or the wind, one frame further on
  }

Plymouth.SetRefreshFunction(refresh_callback);

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one callback we deliberately do NOT use
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plymouth also offers `Plymouth.SetBootProgressFunction`, which is called
# continuously during a normal start-up with its own guess at how far along the
# machine is. A progress bar during an ordinary boot was considered and left out
# on purpose: the pour is the thing that says the machine is alive, and two
# indicators at once is noise. The bar below is only for updates, where somebody
# genuinely wants to know how much longer.


# ==============================================================================
# 7. WRITING WORDS ON THE SCREEN
# ==============================================================================
# `Image.Text` turns a line of words into a picture, which then goes on a sprite
# like any other picture. It needs the plymouth-plugin-label package; without it
# it hands back nothing at all, which is why every use of it is checked before it
# is used rather than assumed to have worked.
#
# `write_centred` is the one helper every piece of text on this screen goes
# through: it draws the words, puts them on the sprite it was handed, and centres
# them on the screen at the height it was given.
fun write_centred (sprite, words, font, red, green, blue, y, z)
  {
    picture = Image.Text(words, red, green, blue, 1, font, "center");
    if (!picture) return;
    sprite.SetImage(picture);
    sprite.SetPosition(screen_middle_x() - picture.GetWidth() / 2, y, z);
    sprite.SetOpacity(1);
  }

# Every piece of text this screen can show gets its own sprite, made once here
# and left empty until there is something to put in it. An empty sprite draws
# nothing.
text.title   = Sprite();   # a heading — "Installing updates", "Unlock the disk"
text.line    = Sprite();   # the line of plain words under it
text.percent = Sprite();   # the percentage on the update screen
text.typed   = Sprite();   # what has been typed, when it is an answer and not
                           # a password (a password shows dots instead)
text.message = Sprite();   # a line the system asked us to show


# ==============================================================================
# 8. THE UPDATE SCREEN
# ==============================================================================
# What the machine shows while it is installing something and must not be turned
# off. The pour plays and holds as usual; this adds the heading, the line of
# explanation, the bar and the percentage underneath it.
#
# The bar is two pictures, one on top of the other: bar-track.png is the empty
# 320x3 line in Midnight's `surfaceAlt`, and bar-fill.png is the same shape in
# Aquarius blue. There is no way to draw a rectangle at boot time, so filling the
# bar means SQUEEZING the blue picture narrower and narrower — `Scale` makes a
# new picture at a new size — and letting the empty one show through behind it.
bar.track = Sprite();
bar.fill = Sprite();
bar.full_picture = Image("bar-fill.png");

fun start_update_screen ()
  {
    if (update.started) return;
    update.started = 1;

    # Which heading, decided by what the machine is doing. Word for word what
    # the boot screen said before this rewrite.
    heading = "Installing updates";
    if (animation.mode == "system-upgrade")   heading = "Upgrading AquariusOS";
    if (animation.mode == "firmware-upgrade") heading = "Updating firmware";
    if (animation.mode == "system-reset")     heading = "Resetting AquariusOS";

    write_centred(text.title, heading, TITLE_FONT,
                  INK_RED, INK_GREEN, INK_BLUE, update.title_y, Z_UPDATE);
    write_centred(text.line, DO_NOT_TURN_OFF, BODY_FONT,
                  SOFT_RED, SOFT_GREEN, SOFT_BLUE, update.line_y, Z_UPDATE);

    track_picture = Image("bar-track.png");
    if (track_picture)
      {
        bar.track.SetImage(track_picture);
        bar.track.SetPosition(update.bar_x, update.bar_y, Z_UPDATE);
        bar.track.SetOpacity(1);
      }
  }

# ⚠️ NOTE THE `part` LOCAL, AND NEVER ASSIGN TO A FUNCTION'S OWN PARAMETER IN
# THIS LANGUAGE. A parameter is not a copy — it is the caller's own value, shared.
# Writing `fraction = 0` here would reach back and change whatever the caller
# handed in, and the day somebody calls this with a variable rather than a sum,
# that variable would silently be set to 0. So the value is copied into a local
# first and the local is what gets tidied up.
fun set_bar (fraction)
  {
    part = fraction;
    if (part < 0) part = 0;
    if (part > 1) part = 1;

    filled = Math.Int(BAR_WIDTH * part);
    if (filled < 1)
      {
        # Nothing done yet. Hide the blue rather than drawing a picture no
        # pixels wide, which is not a thing.
        bar.fill.SetOpacity(0);
        return;
      }
    if (!bar.full_picture) return;

    bar.picture = bar.full_picture.Scale(filled, BAR_HEIGHT);
    bar.fill.SetImage(bar.picture);
    bar.fill.SetPosition(update.bar_x, update.bar_y, Z_UPDATE + 1);
    bar.fill.SetOpacity(1);
  }

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the percentage actually comes from
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Plymouth.SetSystemUpdateFunction` hands us a whole number from 0 to 100. It is
# what `plymouth system-update --progress=NN` sends, and it is what the update
# tooling sends while it works.
#
# ⚠️ THE SCREEN IS DRAWN WHENEVER A PERCENTAGE ARRIVES, whatever mode the machine
# says it is in. That is on purpose and it is what makes the bench test in
# docs/restart/boot-branding.md work: a person can run `plymouth --show-splash`
# and then `plymouth system-update --progress=40` from a terminal on a machine
# that is already up, and see the real update screen. Nothing sends that message
# during an ordinary start-up, so there is no cost to being generous here.
fun system_update_callback (progress)
  {
    start_update_screen();
    set_bar(progress / 100);
    write_centred(text.percent, Math.Int(progress) + "%", NUMBER_FONT,
                  MUTE_RED, MUTE_GREEN, MUTE_BLUE, update.percent_y, Z_UPDATE);

    # Finished. The line changes to say what happens next, and the wind plays —
    # the heading and the full bar stay on screen behind it, so the screen never
    # looks as though it has died.
    if (progress >= 100 && !update.finished)
      {
        update.finished = 1;
        write_centred(text.line, TURNING_OFF, BODY_FONT,
                      SOFT_RED, SOFT_GREEN, SOFT_BLUE, update.line_y, Z_UPDATE);
        load_wind();
        animation.story = "wind";
        animation.frame = 0;
      }
  }

Plymouth.SetSystemUpdateFunction(system_update_callback);


# ==============================================================================
# 9. THE DISK PASSWORD
# ==============================================================================
# On a machine whose disk is encrypted, the computer cannot go any further until
# somebody types the password. This is the screen that asks.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS THE PIECE THAT HAD TO BE WRITTEN BY HAND. The plug-in the boot
# screen used before this rewrite drew a password box for us, in Fedora's plain
# grey. Moving to a plug-in that can play an animation means drawing our own —
# and this is the one screen a build cannot prove is right, because it only
# appears on a machine with an encrypted disk. It is on the bench-test list in
# docs/restart/boot-branding.md for exactly that reason.
#
# The design: a 420x48 box with soft corners in Midnight's `surface`, a hairline
# around it, one dot per character typed, a heading above and a line of plain
# words below. Never Fedora's grey box.
dialog.box = Sprite();
dialog.shown = 0;
dialog.shake_left = 0;
dialog.previous_dots = 0;

fun place_dialog (nudge)
  {
    # `nudge` moves the whole box sideways. It is 0 except during the shake.
    dialog.box.SetPosition(dialog.box_x + nudge, dialog.box_y, Z_DIALOG);
    for (i = 0; dialog.dot[i]; i++)
      dialog.dot[i].SetPosition(dialog.dot_x[i] + nudge, dialog.dot_y, Z_DIALOG + 1);
  }

fun show_dialog (heading, help_words)
  {
    if (!dialog.built)
      {
        picture = Image("box.png");
        if (picture) dialog.box.SetImage(picture);
        dialog.dot_picture = Image("bullet.png");
        # Where the first dot sits: a comfortable margin in from the left edge of
        # the box, and vertically in the middle of it.
        dialog.first_dot_x = dialog.box_x + 20;
        dialog.dot_y = dialog.box_y + BOX_HEIGHT / 2 - 9;
        dialog.built = 1;
      }

    write_centred(text.title, heading, UNLOCK_FONT,
                  INK_RED, INK_GREEN, INK_BLUE, dialog.title_y, Z_DIALOG);
    write_centred(text.line, help_words, BODY_FONT,
                  SOFT_RED, SOFT_GREEN, SOFT_BLUE, dialog.help_y, Z_DIALOG);

    dialog.box.SetOpacity(1);
    dialog.shown = 1;
    place_dialog(0);
  }

fun hide_dialog ()
  {
    dialog.box.SetOpacity(0);
    for (i = 0; dialog.dot[i]; i++)
      dialog.dot[i].SetOpacity(0);
    text.title.SetOpacity(0);
    text.line.SetOpacity(0);
    text.typed.SetOpacity(0);
    dialog.shown = 0;
    dialog.previous_dots = 0;
    dialog.shake_left = 0;
  }

# One dot per character typed. Dots are made the first time they are needed and
# then reused — somebody who types twelve characters, deletes them and types
# eight again gets the same twelve dots back, eight of them visible.
#
# ⚠️ WITH A CEILING. The box is 420 pixels wide, the dots start 20 pixels in and
# sit 18 apart, so 21 of them reach the far edge. A real disk passphrase is
# often longer than that, and without this the dots would march straight out of
# the box and off across the screen. Twenty is the most that are ever shown; the
# typing still works exactly as before, it just stops adding dots.
MAX_DOTS = 20;

fun set_dots (how_many)
  {
    if (!dialog.dot_picture) return;

    # A local, not the parameter: see the warning above set_bar.
    visible = how_many;
    if (visible > MAX_DOTS) visible = MAX_DOTS;

    for (i = 0; dialog.dot[i] || i < visible; i++)
      {
        if (!dialog.dot[i])
          {
            dialog.dot[i] = Sprite(dialog.dot_picture);
            dialog.dot_x[i] = dialog.first_dot_x + i * 18;
          }
        if (i < visible)
          dialog.dot[i].SetOpacity(1);
        else
          dialog.dot[i].SetOpacity(0);
      }
    place_dialog(0);
  }

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shake, when a password is wrong
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One step of it per frame, seven frames in all. `refresh_callback` above calls
# this on every single frame; it does nothing unless a shake is running.
fun step_shake ()
  {
    if (dialog.shake_left < 1) return;
    step = SHAKE_STEPS - dialog.shake_left;
    place_dialog(shake_offset[step]);
    dialog.shake_left--;
  }

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plymouth calls this every time the password box should be shown — once when it
# first appears, and again after every single keystroke, with the number of
# characters typed so far.
#
# ⚠️ HOW WE KNOW A PASSWORD WAS WRONG. Plymouth does not tell us. What it does is
# ask again, from the beginning, with the count back at zero. So: if the box was
# already up, the last count was more than zero, and the new count is zero, the
# attempt failed. That is the only signal there is, and it is what every other
# boot theme in the world uses.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
fun display_password_callback (prompt, dots)
  {
    heading = UNLOCK_TITLE;
    help_words = UNLOCK_HELP;

    if (dialog.shown && dialog.previous_dots > 0 && dots == 0)
      {
        help_words = UNLOCK_WRONG;
        dialog.shake_left = SHAKE_STEPS;
      }

    show_dialog(heading, help_words);
    set_dots(dots);
    dialog.previous_dots = dots;
  }

Plymouth.SetDisplayPasswordFunction(display_password_callback);


# ==============================================================================
# 10. QUESTIONS, MESSAGES, AND GOING BACK TO NORMAL
# ==============================================================================
# A QUESTION is the rare case where the machine wants words rather than a
# password — so what is typed is shown rather than hidden behind dots. The
# question itself comes from the system, so it is shown as it was given rather
# than replaced with words of our own.
fun display_question_callback (prompt, typed_so_far)
  {
    show_dialog(prompt, ANSWER_HELP);
    set_dots(0);
    write_centred(text.typed, typed_so_far, BODY_FONT,
                  INK_RED, INK_GREEN, INK_BLUE,
                  dialog.box_y + BOX_HEIGHT / 2 - 12, Z_DIALOG + 1);
  }

Plymouth.SetDisplayQuestionFunction(display_question_callback);

# A MESSAGE is a line the system wants a person to read — usually something has
# gone wrong and is being explained. It goes at the very bottom, in front of
# everything else, and stays until the system takes it away again.
fun display_message_callback (words)
  {
    write_centred(text.message, words, BODY_FONT,
                  SOFT_RED, SOFT_GREEN, SOFT_BLUE,
                  Window.GetY() + Window.GetHeight() - 64, Z_MESSAGE);
  }

fun hide_message_callback (words)
  {
    text.message.SetOpacity(0);
  }

# `SetDisplayMessageFunction` is the name in this version of Plymouth.
# `SetMessageFunction` is an older name for the same thing and still works; the
# current one is used here so that a search of Plymouth's own source finds it.
Plymouth.SetDisplayMessageFunction(display_message_callback);
Plymouth.SetHideMessageFunction(hide_message_callback);

# NORMAL means the machine has stopped asking for anything: the password was
# accepted, or the question was answered. Everything the dialog put on screen
# goes away and the mark is left holding on its own.
fun display_normal_callback ()
  {
    hide_dialog();
    text.typed.SetOpacity(0);
  }

Plymouth.SetDisplayNormalFunction(display_normal_callback);

# QUIT is Plymouth handing over — to the login screen, or to the machine going
# off. There is nothing to tidy up: whatever is on screen at that moment is
# exactly what should be on screen, and Plymouth takes it away itself.
fun quit_callback ()
  {
  }

Plymouth.SetQuitFunction(quit_callback);
